/* ============================================================
   LANE CIRCUIT REVAMP — the vertical lane borders are one
   continuous line from the enemy banner edge down to the player
   banner edge. The line reads the banner colour at each end:
     top  = --enemy-accent-bright  (red default, blue when player picks red)
     bot  = --player-accent-bright (the picked theme)
   Middle stays neutral white for an empty lane; occupancy states
   blend the current through the whole column.
   ============================================================ */
.lane {
  border-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
      var(--enemy-accent-bright)   0%,
      var(--enemy-accent-bright)   2%,
      rgba(255,255,255,0.90)       9%,
      rgba(255,255,255,0.90)       91%,
      var(--player-accent-bright)  98%,
      var(--player-accent-bright)  100%
    ) 1 !important;
}
.lane:hover {
  border-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
      var(--enemy-accent-bright)   0%,
      var(--enemy-accent-bright)   2%,
      var(--player-accent-bright)  9%,
      var(--player-accent-bright)  91%,
      var(--player-accent-bright)  98%,
      var(--player-accent-bright)  100%
    ) 1 !important;
  box-shadow:
    0 0 12px var(--player-glow-outer),
    0 0 24px var(--player-glow-inner) !important;
}
/* AI owns — enemy colour flows the full column with a blend kiss where
   it meets the player banner. */
.lane.occ-ai {
  border-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
      var(--enemy-accent-bright)  0%,
      var(--enemy-accent-bright)  91%,
      color-mix(in srgb,
        rgb(var(--enemy-r), var(--enemy-g), var(--enemy-b)) 50%,
        rgb(var(--player-r), var(--player-g), var(--player-b))) 97%,
      var(--player-accent-bright) 100%
    ) 1 !important;
  box-shadow:
    0 0 12px var(--enemy-glow-outer),
    0 0 24px var(--enemy-glow-inner) !important;
}
/* Player owns — mirror. */
.lane.occ-player {
  border-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
      var(--enemy-accent-bright)  0%,
      color-mix(in srgb,
        rgb(var(--enemy-r), var(--enemy-g), var(--enemy-b)) 50%,
        rgb(var(--player-r), var(--player-g), var(--player-b))) 3%,
      var(--player-accent-bright) 9%,
      var(--player-accent-bright) 100%
    ) 1 !important;
  box-shadow:
    0 0 12px var(--player-glow-outer),
    0 0 24px var(--player-glow-inner) !important;
}
/* Contested — real gradient, blended middle, no hardcoded purple. */
/* CONTESTED LANE BORDER — smooth red top half → trick-purple middle
   → cyan bottom half. The same gradient applies to BOTH seams of a
   contested lane (the lane's own left border AND the next lane's
   left border which forms its right seam). Symmetry across the
   lane's verticals so an inside contested lane reads as "framed by
   the contested gradient on both sides." Bumped specificity to
   (0,2,1) via the `.board` ancestor so this rule wins over the
   `.lane + .lane` empty gradient + `.lane:hover + .lane` hover
   gradient (both at 0,2,0). User spec: "how are we handling the
   inside lanes?" — answer: now the right seam of a contested
   lane mirrors its left so inside contested lanes are framed
   symmetrically. */
/* Theme-aware contested lane — uses var(--contest-pop) for the
   midpoint so the seam color is hand-tuned per theme (vivid neon
   instead of muddy sRGB-average). User direction: "make the middle
   POP." Per-theme blends:
     • cyan default + red → vivid magenta
     • silver/white + red → HOT PINK
     • gold + red → vivid orange
     • green + red → bright yellow (the warm-color complement)
     • purple + red → vivid magenta
   Selector list explicitly covers :first-child and :last-child
   variants to win over the `.board .lane:last-child` rule (which
   has specificity 0,2,1 — same as ours; without :child we'd lose
   on source-order). */
/* (REMOVED — same rationale as the occ-ai/occ-player adjacency
   rules above. The contested (occ-both) lane no longer paints
   its neighbors; each lane renders its own borders via the
   --lane-mid-color system. The contested lane itself gets the
   color-mix() blend in its OWN border, which IS the story. */
/* (Reverted: this slot held a `.lane.occ-both + .lane` rule that
   painted the right-side seam with a banded purple gradient. User
   asked for the lane borders to look "like they were before" — so
   the right seam now falls back to the default `.lane + .lane`
   empty-gradient stack. Only the CENTERLINE remains purple. */
/* Base empty lane glow — was 12/24 at 0.35 alpha which was bleeding
   between adjacent lanes into a white wash. Lower + tighter. */
.lane {
  box-shadow:
    0 0 6px rgba(255,255,255,0.15),
    0 0 12px rgba(255,255,255,0.06) !important;
}

/* ============================================================
   LANE POLISH — beam-of-light backgrounds + + placeholder hidden
   + rarity pip revert (hand/draft stays rarity, only board themes)
   ============================================================ */

/* Hide the "+" placeholder glyph per user request — looks cheap. */
.empty-lane-glyph { display: none !important; }

/* ============================================================
   SINGLE LANE DIVIDER + BOARD EDGE CLEANUP
   Adjacent lanes were rendering two 1px borders butting up — a
   visibly thicker 2px seam. Hiding the right-side border on every
   lane except the last collapses each inter-lane seam to one line.
   Also drops the outer-left border of lane 1 and outer-right of
   lane 6 (via the same rule) so the board doesn't show phantom
   white lines flanking the row. The corner brackets on
   .board-section are also removed — they were floating outside
   the lanes where the user flagged "a weird white/red glow".
   ============================================================ */
/* Every lane gets a LEFT border (paints both the outer frame edge of
   lane 1 AND each inter-lane seam between lanes 2-6). Right border is
   removed except on the LAST lane (frames the right edge of the
   board). User flag: "the left border line isn't showing up" — root
   cause: the previous `.board .lane:first-child` rule never matched
   because `#board` has `.round-watermark` and 4 `.board-mote` divs
   as its first 5 children, so the first .lane is actually the 6th
   child of #board and `:first-child` never resolved. Just unifying
   border-left on all lanes side-steps the problem entirely. */
.lane {
  border-right: none !important;
  border-left: 1px solid transparent !important;
  border-image: linear-gradient(180deg,
      var(--enemy-accent-bright)   0%,
      var(--enemy-accent-bright)   2%,
      rgba(255,255,255,0.90)       9%,
      rgba(255,255,255,0.90)       91%,
      var(--player-accent-bright)  98%,
      var(--player-accent-bright)  100%
    ) 1 !important;
}
/* (REMOVED — these legacy adjacent-lane painting rules were the
   cause of "lane 2 lit up with red" when lane 1 has an enemy.
   They painted the adjacent lane's border with the occupied
   lane's color, which violated the user's color-story spec:
   "if there's nobody contesting the lane, it should be all white...
   when I hover over lane one, lane two is also highlighting."
   Each lane now paints ITS OWN borders via .board-section .lane —
   no neighbor influence. If two adjacent lanes are both occupied,
   each shows its own color independently. The 2px combined
   thickness at adjacent-lane seams is acceptable and crisp. */
/* (Reverted: previous experiment painted the right seam solid purple,
   but that broke the lane separation the user wanted. The seam to the
   right of a contested lane now falls back to the next lane's own
   default occupancy / hover styling — exactly how it looked before
   the contested-purple experiment. Only the CENTERLINE is purple
   now, not the vertical border seams.) */
/* (Sibling-lane hover paint REMOVED per user feedback: "when I
   hover over lane one, lane two is also highlighting. Which is
   kind of distracting." The original `.lane:hover + .lane` rule
   here painted the adjacent lane's border alongside the hovered
   one — clean visual idea but read as a bug to the player. Only
   the actually-hovered lane should respond.) */

/* Kill the .board-section corner brackets (they framed outside
   lane 1 / lane 6 and read as disconnected chrome). ::before is
   repurposed below as the round-start sweep overlay; only ::after
   is hidden here. */
.board-section::after { display: none !important; }
/* Also drop the heavy inset banner-bleed on the board-section —
   the banner borders themselves already carry that glow, and the
   inset was painting the extra "left of lane 1" white/red artifact
   the user flagged. */
.board-section {
  box-shadow: none !important;
  padding: 0 !important;
}

/* Tricks section — use a full-width theme-accent line above the
   tricks row instead of the muted grey-cyan hybrid it had. */
.tricks-section {
  padding-top: 12px !important;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--theme-accent-edge) !important;
  box-shadow: 0 -1px 0 var(--theme-glow-inner) !important;
}
.player-hand-section {
